236
00:10:17,360 --> 00:10:20,080
We're speaking to Alex from Bozeman,

237
00:10:20,120 --> 00:10:21,600
and he's also the author

238
00:10:21,600 --> 00:10:24,560
of the American Mountain Club's Guide to Outdoor Leadership,

239
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:27,120
which, if you haven't seen it yet, folks,

240
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:28,640
it's a great resource.

241
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:30,520
The real foundation of the book

242
00:10:30,520 --> 00:10:34,800
is in your definition of good outdoor leadership.

243
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:36,000
What can you tell me about that?

244
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,560
Well, the way I define it is that outdoor leadership

245
00:10:38,560 --> 00:10:42,040
has three kind of foundational components,

246
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:44,400
And in the book I kind of use the metaphor

247
00:10:44,400 --> 00:10:46,360
of a three-legged stool

248
00:10:46,360 --> 00:10:48,960
and each leg is important for supporting

249
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:51,960
your overall outdoor leadership ability.

250
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:56,560
But those legs or foundations are technical skills,

251
00:10:56,560 --> 00:11:00,200
interpersonal skills and judgment skills.

252
00:11:00,200 --> 00:11:01,560
And technical skills are what

253
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:04,840
that's: your ability to know how to set up tents

254
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:08,840
and cook food and navigate and all those kind of things.

255
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:12,080
Interpersonal skills are the ability

256
00:11:12,080 --> 00:11:15,640
to work with other leaders

257
00:11:15,640 --> 00:11:19,280
and with members of your group in a harmonious way.

258
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:20,840
And then judgment skills:

259
00:11:20,840 --> 00:11:24,760
the ability to see what's going on around you,

260
00:11:24,760 --> 00:11:28,319
both in the environment and in your group

261
00:11:28,319 --> 00:11:29,920
and even with yourself,

262
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:33,240
and be able to assess what's going on

263
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:35,760
and make good decisions.

264
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:39,840
And I certainly focus on safety in the book,

265
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:43,080
but it's also that judgment also relates

266
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:46,680
to producing a good outcome,

267
00:11:46,680 --> 00:11:48,720
a good educational experience

268
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:50,320
or a good overall experience

269
00:11:50,320 --> 00:11:52,960
for the participants in your group as well.

270
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:54,640
The judgment piece:

271
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:58,080
you can learn some things about it through reading-

272
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:01,440
and that it's mostly an experience-based skill,

273
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:02,280
wouldn't you say

274
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:03,120
Absolutely?

275
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:05,720
And that's what I say in the book-

276
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:07,800
is that this book it's a framework,

277
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:09,920
it's a starting point,

278
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:11,560
but the best leaders are the ones

279
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:14,320
who are getting that hands-on experience

280
00:12:14,320 --> 00:12:18,000
and developing those judgment skills based on experience.

281
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,120
That said, I do think there's room in there

282
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:21,800
for developing those judgment skills

283
00:12:21,800 --> 00:12:24,800
through really good training programs

284
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:28,280
And some of the Wilderness First Aid type courses

285
00:12:28,280 --> 00:12:30,800
and leadership courses out there

286
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:35,600
that try and put you in simulations or scenarios

287
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:38,040
can really help develop some of those judgment skills.

288
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:40,000
But at the end of the day,

289
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,400
nothing beats experience working with and leading groups

290
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:47,079
in the outdoors for building up those judgment skills.

291
00:12:47,079 --> 00:12:50,520
Let's talk about this Wilderness First Responder course.

292
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:54,599
I was able to take that and I thought it was great.

293
00:12:54,599 --> 00:12:55,680
It was fairly easy.

294
00:12:55,680 --> 00:12:58,520
I did it over a course of two Saturdays a year or so.

295
00:12:58,520 --> 00:13:01,480
How would you encourage somebody to go and take that course?

296
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:05,000
I think that the two-day Wilderness First Aid course

297
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,880
is just absolutely critical

298
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:10,880
for anyone who's venturing outside of the reach

299
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:15,880
of the quick response from the 911 and EMS network.

300
00:13:16,160 --> 00:13:20,520
I've dealt with quite a few backcountry emergencies,

301
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:21,760
some of them life-threatening,

302
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:26,200
and it's so important to be able to fall back

303
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:30,160
to that basic framework about what to think about first.

304
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:31,840
what are your steps in your process

305
00:13:31,840 --> 00:13:33,960
of dealing with that situation?

306
00:13:33,960 --> 00:13:36,120
I encourage people to take that course.

307
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:37,040
whenever I can

308
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:40,760
You go on in the new edition of the book

309
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:42,400
and elaborate on some techniques

310
00:13:42,400 --> 00:13:44,320
that can be used with teenagers.

311
00:13:44,320 --> 00:13:46,800
The thing that is really important to me

312
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:49,320
is that the outdoor experience

313
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:51,160
be something that's different

314
00:13:51,160 --> 00:13:54,160
from teenagers' normal life.

315
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:59,160
If you go out there and it's kind of a situation

316
00:14:01,640 --> 00:14:03,680
that they experience much like they're at home,

317
00:14:03,720 --> 00:14:07,800
or teachers or parents are always telling them what to do,

318
00:14:07,800 --> 00:14:10,200
it's not gonna be nearly as valuable

319
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:12,720
as if you give the teenagers

320
00:14:12,720 --> 00:14:14,640
some progressive responsibility

321
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:19,400
to take on ownership of the activity,

322
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:21,560
And I think that's something that's really missing

323
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:24,600
for a lot of youth today,

324
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:27,240
And so what I'm suggesting is

325
00:14:27,240 --> 00:14:32,040
that progressively turn over responsibility for the trip

326
00:14:32,040 --> 00:14:36,599
so that teens are really both responsible for things

327
00:14:36,599 --> 00:14:38,319
which they aren't always.

328
00:14:38,319 --> 00:14:40,640
So they have to pack their own canoe,

329
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:41,719
cook their own food,

330
00:14:41,719 --> 00:14:43,079
and if they don't do a good job,

331
00:14:43,079 --> 00:14:44,640
their consequences.

332
00:14:44,640 --> 00:14:47,400
they take over more responsibility

333
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:51,480
And eventually, by the end of a week-long trip,

334
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:54,880
they may be doing all the navigation

335
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:58,640
and you as a leader may be sort of watching

336
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,319
from a distance to make sure

337
00:15:00,319 --> 00:15:03,760
that nothing goes catastrophically wrong,

338
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:07,080
but giving them a little bit of a leeway

339
00:15:07,080 --> 00:15:10,000
to make some mistakes that aren't gonna be

340
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,560
have any real harm or safety repercussions

341
00:15:13,560 --> 00:15:16,640
And I think that that experience

342
00:15:16,640 --> 00:15:20,080
is something that is really valuable

343
00:15:20,080 --> 00:15:22,319
in terms of building interest in the outdoors,

344
00:15:22,319 --> 00:15:25,600
but also building overall life skills,

345
00:15:25,640 --> 00:15:29,280
decision-making skills for the teenagers

346
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:32,720
And, ultimately, the activities we're doing in the outdoors.

347
00:15:32,720 --> 00:15:35,120
there's some safety issues,

348
00:15:35,120 --> 00:15:37,400
there's some skill involved,

349
00:15:37,400 --> 00:15:39,920
but they aren't that complicated.

350
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:42,480
They're a fairly discreet set of skills

351
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:45,800
and you can train people on those pretty quickly,

352
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:47,600
especially if they're not-

353
00:15:47,600 --> 00:15:51,760
we're not talking about climbing glaciated mountains,

354
00:15:51,760 --> 00:15:54,120
but the kind of basic activities

355
00:15:54,120 --> 00:15:55,480
we do in the outdoors.

356
00:15:55,520 --> 00:15:57,120
The other thing I think is important

357
00:15:57,120 --> 00:16:01,440
is kind of finding the right balance of challenge and reward,

358
00:16:01,440 --> 00:16:03,160
And I've been with groups

359
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:05,280
and I've seen other groups out there on the trail

360
00:16:05,280 --> 00:16:08,840
where they just kind of on these huge marches

361
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:10,520
and that can be rewarding

362
00:16:10,520 --> 00:16:12,920
if everyone's bought into the idea

363
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:17,320
of accomplishing a lot of miles and getting something done.

364
00:16:17,320 --> 00:16:21,080
but sometimes giving the group a chance

365
00:16:21,080 --> 00:16:23,680
to climb a peak with an amazing view

366
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:26,000
and having a real feeling of success

367
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,320
that way may be more important

368
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:31,040
than pushing them really, really hard.

369
00:16:31,040 --> 00:16:34,040
or having the opportunity to have a campfire at night

370
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:37,280
after a solid day of paddling

371
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:41,320
is more important than getting in your 30 mile paddle that day.

372
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:43,640
So there's this fine balance of figuring out

373
00:16:43,640 --> 00:16:46,440
what's the appropriate amount of challenge for a group

374
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:51,440
and how can the activities that you're doing be rewarding,

375
00:16:51,880 --> 00:16:53,880
either through a sense of accomplishment

376
00:16:53,880 --> 00:16:56,760
or also some kind of other view

377
00:16:56,760 --> 00:17:01,280
or a fun night around the campfire or something like that?

378
00:17:01,280 --> 00:17:04,520
So it's not all about covering miles,

379
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:06,520
No, I mean I think that some of the groups

380
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:10,240
that have gotten the most that I've led,

381
00:17:10,240 --> 00:17:11,960
that have gotten the most out of it,

382
00:17:11,960 --> 00:17:14,160
have had their challenging days

383
00:17:14,160 --> 00:17:15,440
and they've had to push a bit,

384
00:17:15,440 --> 00:17:17,040
but they've also had easier days

385
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:18,400
where there were a lot of fun things:

386
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:22,080
swimming the lake, fun things to do out there too,

387
00:17:22,080 --> 00:17:25,560
and a chance to bond with teenagers

388
00:17:25,560 --> 00:17:27,640
in a way, other teens in a way

389
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:31,560
that they don't always get to do at home.

390
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:33,240
There's a bit of advice that you give

391
00:17:33,240 --> 00:17:37,000
that you may be unaware of that. it's pretty unique,

392
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,680
but I haven't seen it anywhere else

393
00:17:39,680 --> 00:17:41,440
other than in your book

394
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:44,000
and that, especially when working with teenagers,

395
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,080
is feeding them

396
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:50,320
And frequent snacks and things like that.

397
00:17:50,320 --> 00:17:54,639
I mean that actually never really occurred to me.

398
00:17:54,639 --> 00:17:55,960
What can you tell me about that?

399
00:17:55,960 --> 00:17:57,439
I think it's brilliant.

400
00:17:57,439 --> 00:17:59,320
Well, it's something that

401
00:17:59,320 --> 00:18:01,560
I mean- I've heard this from other people,

402
00:18:01,560 --> 00:18:03,720
but I've also had this experience-

403
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:07,040
The group morale and motivation and energy

404
00:18:07,040 --> 00:18:10,919
just sort of precipitously dropping

405
00:18:10,919 --> 00:18:12,360
and all of a sudden kids

406
00:18:12,360 --> 00:18:14,600
who are really excited to be there,

407
00:18:14,600 --> 00:18:15,879
they're not anymore.

408
00:18:15,880 --> 00:18:17,800
It took me a while to put it together,

409
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:21,280
but often some food will really

410
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:23,440
really help to solve that situation

411
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:25,320
and the science behind that is

412
00:18:25,320 --> 00:18:28,480
teenagers have really really high metabolism levels

413
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:31,640
and they need to eat a lot more frequently

414
00:18:31,640 --> 00:18:33,160
than most adults do

415
00:18:33,160 --> 00:18:34,920
to keep up their blood sugar levels.

416
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:37,720
and when those blood sugar levels drop,

417
00:18:37,720 --> 00:18:40,520
attitudes really go south.

418
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:44,560
It has a huge impact on the ability of the group

419
00:18:44,600 --> 00:18:47,000
to work together and get things done.

420
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:50,919
The key there is that if you're at all hungry,

421
00:18:50,919 --> 00:18:54,639
your group is almost certainly hungrier than you are

422
00:18:54,639 --> 00:18:57,360
and maybe not even be that tuned into that.

423
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:01,280
but making sure people have the opportunity to say,

424
00:19:01,280 --> 00:19:02,760
hey, I need a snack,

425
00:19:02,760 --> 00:19:06,520
and even encouraging that before people bring it up,

426
00:19:06,520 --> 00:19:10,000
is a great way to keep the group moving.

427
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,080
Just feed and hydrate your group.

428
00:19:12,120 --> 00:19:15,360
It's not just the food. the water helps a lot too.

429
00:19:15,360 --> 00:19:18,960
It's one of the most basic but helpful tips

430
00:19:18,960 --> 00:19:21,040
I've ever come across

431
00:19:21,040 --> 00:19:23,919
And I gotta tell you I know this.

432
00:19:23,919 --> 00:19:27,280
I think I knew this at some level,

433
00:19:27,280 --> 00:19:29,600
but it was never like a part of the plan.

434
00:19:29,600 --> 00:19:30,439
Right,

435
00:19:30,439 --> 00:19:33,960
And that is worth the price of admission on the book alone.

436
00:19:36,560 --> 00:19:39,000
Well, it's just hugely important. and you're right,

437
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:40,879
All of us who've done any work

438
00:19:40,880 --> 00:19:45,520
with scouts have encountered this in one way or another.

439
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:48,240
All of a sudden, everybody just kind of hits the wall

440
00:19:48,240 --> 00:19:51,160
and they start dropping like flies

441
00:19:51,160 --> 00:19:54,960
and you're wondering what the heck is going on.

442
00:19:54,960 --> 00:19:58,320
and it's just a granola bar away, really,

443
00:19:58,320 --> 00:19:59,160
Exactly.

444
00:20:00,240 --> 00:20:03,800
Another piece of that, too, is that teenagers,

445
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:08,800
especially males, have incredibly high appetites.

446
00:20:10,880 --> 00:20:13,360
They can really really eat a lot of food,

447
00:20:13,360 --> 00:20:17,120
and most American boys are used to getting

448
00:20:17,120 --> 00:20:19,200
all the food that they want.

449
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:20,800
And, of course, if you're backpacking,

450
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:22,680
you have to carry that food.

451
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:25,160
But my advice in general is,

452
00:20:25,160 --> 00:20:27,960
especially when you're packing for teenage boys,

453
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:29,920
is: pack on the heavier side.

454
00:20:29,920 --> 00:20:32,920
You're better off having to carry a little bit more

455
00:20:32,920 --> 00:20:37,920
than to have kids who are complaining about being hungry

456
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:40,680
and potentially really are hungry

457
00:20:40,680 --> 00:20:44,480
and don't have the food to keep themselves well fueled.

458
00:20:44,480 --> 00:20:47,480
And they don't normally associate hunger

459
00:20:47,480 --> 00:20:50,720
with a drop in morale or a drop in behavior.

460
00:20:50,720 --> 00:20:52,560
They just get cranky,

461
00:20:52,560 --> 00:20:53,400
Exactly.

462
00:20:53,400 --> 00:20:55,440
They're not even aware,

463
00:20:55,440 --> 00:20:58,600
well, I'm hungry. that makes me cranky,

464
00:20:58,600 --> 00:21:01,000
makes me less effective at what I'm doing.

465
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:02,840
They just go there,

466
00:21:02,840 --> 00:21:04,000
Yep.

467
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:07,720
So being able to notice that is pretty golden.

468
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:11,280
One of the things that I really found useful

469
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:14,840
is your discussion on expedition behavior.

470
00:21:14,840 --> 00:21:17,200
Why is expedition behavior different

471
00:21:17,200 --> 00:21:20,000
from evening meetings that we have and things like that?

472
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:21,080
What makes it unique?

473
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:23,040
The idea behind expedition behavior

474
00:21:23,040 --> 00:21:26,800
is that you're really interdependent upon each other

475
00:21:26,800 --> 00:21:30,000
and the group isn't going to function well

476
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:31,640
if people aren't working together

477
00:21:31,640 --> 00:21:34,680
in someone of a harmonious way.

478
00:21:34,720 --> 00:21:38,400
It really comes out of kind of expeditionary climbing,

479
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:42,680
like climbing big heat peaks in the Himalayas or in Alaska,

480
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:44,840
but the concepts are very applicable

481
00:21:44,840 --> 00:21:49,160
to any outdoor kind of experience,

482
00:21:49,160 --> 00:21:51,840
especially multi-day ones. we go on it,

483
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:55,480
But it sort of begins with the idea of self-awareness,

484
00:21:55,480 --> 00:21:58,080
so being aware of what your own needs are,

485
00:21:58,080 --> 00:22:00,120
And then it flows into the idea

486
00:22:00,120 --> 00:22:02,920
of being able taking responsibility

487
00:22:02,920 --> 00:22:04,840
for taking care of your own needs.

488
00:22:04,840 --> 00:22:09,400
A classic example is somebody goes off to dinner

489
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:13,120
and leaves their sleeping bag out and it starts to rain

490
00:22:13,120 --> 00:22:15,400
and then their sleeping bag is wet

491
00:22:15,400 --> 00:22:18,000
And that has an impact on the entire group

492
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:21,840
because they didn't take on their own kind of self-leadership

493
00:22:21,840 --> 00:22:25,520
and taking care of their own things.

494
00:22:25,520 --> 00:22:27,160
And then it moves on from there

495
00:22:27,160 --> 00:22:31,720
to the idea of being kind of supportive of group goals,

496
00:22:31,720 --> 00:22:36,080
So committed to group goals, tolerant of each other.

497
00:22:36,080 --> 00:22:39,760
You know people on- I mean, I'm sure you've had this experience.

498
00:22:39,760 --> 00:22:41,840
people come into these things

499
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:45,000
with different comfort levels for being in the outdoors

500
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:48,680
and different physical ability levels

501
00:22:48,680 --> 00:22:52,600
And it's often easy for those who are more comfortable

502
00:22:52,600 --> 00:22:56,480
or physically able to be let's see like critical

503
00:22:56,480 --> 00:22:59,880
or not supportive of those who are less comfortable.

504
00:22:59,880 --> 00:23:02,800
But if people are supportive within the group

505
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:05,840
and committed to the group goals,

506
00:23:05,840 --> 00:23:09,080
that support can really really help those people

507
00:23:09,080 --> 00:23:10,760
who are struggling through,

508
00:23:10,760 --> 00:23:13,360
can make them feel more a part of the group

509
00:23:13,360 --> 00:23:17,400
and it can help them to really overcome

510
00:23:17,400 --> 00:23:20,520
some of those challenges and make the experience a success.

511
00:23:20,520 --> 00:23:23,360
And another piece of that whole expedition behavior

512
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:27,200
is good communication and being able

513
00:23:27,200 --> 00:23:32,040
to express yourself in a supportive, non-critical way

514
00:23:32,040 --> 00:23:33,760
with other members of the group.

515
00:23:33,760 --> 00:23:36,280
So this is the expedition behavior concept,

516
00:23:36,280 --> 00:23:40,160
is something that leaders really need to have in themselves,

517
00:23:40,160 --> 00:23:45,160
but it's something that the whole group can really commit to.

518
00:23:45,280 --> 00:23:47,320
And one of the things that I like to do

519
00:23:47,320 --> 00:23:50,720
with groups of teenagers particularly

520
00:23:50,720 --> 00:23:54,160
is introduce this kind of concept of expedition behavior-

521
00:23:54,160 --> 00:23:56,480
or you can call it something else if you want.

522
00:23:56,480 --> 00:23:58,720
So you're going to have a group early on

523
00:23:58,720 --> 00:24:01,280
and have the group help to define

524
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:04,200
what the expectations of each other are

525
00:24:04,200 --> 00:24:05,920
And you might have to prompt them a little.

526
00:24:05,920 --> 00:24:08,760
Do you say, do you want people to be supportive

527
00:24:08,760 --> 00:24:09,720
when you're having a hard time

528
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:11,440
or do you want them to be critical?

529
00:24:11,440 --> 00:24:13,440
And they're inevitably,

530
00:24:13,440 --> 00:24:15,160
especially within the scouting community,

531
00:24:15,160 --> 00:24:19,280
they're going to understand the value of being supportive

532
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:21,880
And that early commitment from the group

533
00:24:21,880 --> 00:24:25,240
to those concepts can be really helpful later on,

534
00:24:25,560 --> 00:24:29,240
kind of say: hey remember, early in the trip

535
00:24:29,240 --> 00:24:31,920
we talked about expedition behavior

536
00:24:31,920 --> 00:24:33,600
and being supportive of one another

537
00:24:33,600 --> 00:24:34,760
when we're having a hard time,

538
00:24:34,760 --> 00:24:37,520
And for a lot of teens that'll really click

539
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:41,520
and they say: oh yeah, Joe's having a hard time,

540
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:45,840
Now's not the time to complain about him slowing us down,

541
00:24:45,840 --> 00:24:48,680
Now's the time to maybe take a little weight from him

542
00:24:48,680 --> 00:24:51,200
and joke with him a little and try and cheer him up.

543
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:53,200
You hit on something else that I think

544
00:24:53,240 --> 00:24:55,160
in their limited awareness,

545
00:24:55,160 --> 00:24:58,040
teenagers don't always realize:

546
00:24:58,040 --> 00:24:59,560
if they're uncomfortable

547
00:24:59,560 --> 00:25:02,320
or if they're physically incapable of doing something,

548
00:25:02,320 --> 00:25:05,040
they get kind of afraid and they get very reluctant

549
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:07,080
and they get really recalcitrant-

550
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:09,160
I think that's the word I'm looking for-

551
00:25:09,160 --> 00:25:12,280
about moving ahead or trying to do anything.

552
00:25:12,280 --> 00:25:13,960
And I don't think that they associate-

553
00:25:13,960 --> 00:25:16,920
just like they don't associate- being cranky

554
00:25:16,920 --> 00:25:21,920
or difficult with not having enough to eat or drink.

555
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,840
They don't associate the fact that they're kind of afraid

556
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:26,560
or uncomfortable with something

557
00:25:26,560 --> 00:25:28,840
with the way that they behave.

558
00:25:28,840 --> 00:25:32,880
Understanding that and kind of inculcating in the group

559
00:25:32,880 --> 00:25:35,840
these expedition behaviors that you're talking about,

560
00:25:35,840 --> 00:25:37,800
you're gonna avoid an awful lot of that,

561
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:41,360
Absolutely. I mean, that's so critically important

562
00:25:41,360 --> 00:25:44,280
is I really believe in outdoor experiences

563
00:25:44,280 --> 00:25:47,480
and a kind of concept of challenge by choice,

564
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:51,880
So that you shouldn't be out there forcing people

565
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:54,600
to do something that they don't wanna do,

566
00:25:54,600 --> 00:25:56,080
Although there might be situations

567
00:25:56,080 --> 00:25:58,480
where you have to get through a bunch of miles

568
00:25:58,480 --> 00:26:00,400
because you have to finish the trip.

569
00:26:00,400 --> 00:26:02,120
But for the most part,

570
00:26:02,120 --> 00:26:05,440
if that group can be supportive of one another,

571
00:26:05,440 --> 00:26:07,760
you really avoid those situations

572
00:26:07,760 --> 00:26:09,720
where you call that recalcitrant,

573
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:13,680
where teens get really reluctant to do stuff

574
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:16,440
And often you're right, they cannot verbalize

575
00:26:16,440 --> 00:26:20,120
I'm scared, I'm tired, I'm hungry.

576
00:26:20,120 --> 00:26:23,000
But if the group's really supporting each other

577
00:26:23,000 --> 00:26:26,800
and the teens- the boys are having an easier time,

578
00:26:26,800 --> 00:26:30,360
are really supportive and encouraging

579
00:26:30,360 --> 00:26:32,880
and helpful to those who are having a harder time,

580
00:26:32,880 --> 00:26:37,080
the net accomplishments the group can achieve

581
00:26:37,080 --> 00:26:39,440
are so, so, so much higher

582
00:26:39,440 --> 00:26:43,320
than if you let the people who are having a hard time

583
00:26:43,320 --> 00:26:46,600
drag at the back of the pack

584
00:26:46,600 --> 00:26:49,280
and feel miserable for themselves.

585
00:26:49,280 --> 00:26:52,480
It's really, really crucial to understand these dynamics

586
00:26:52,480 --> 00:26:54,080
and then to put them to work.

587
00:26:54,080 --> 00:26:57,080
So let's spend a little time just going over

588
00:26:57,080 --> 00:26:59,080
some of the techniques that you have in the book

589
00:26:59,080 --> 00:27:02,280
as far as leaders and group development goes.

590
00:27:02,280 --> 00:27:03,879
In encouraging group development,

591
00:27:03,879 --> 00:27:06,480
it's helpful to understand that there's

592
00:27:06,480 --> 00:27:09,399
kind of a natural group development process

593
00:27:09,399 --> 00:27:11,680
that most groups go through

594
00:27:11,680 --> 00:27:13,200
when they're on an outdoor experience,

595
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:14,600
And there's some variation,

596
00:27:14,600 --> 00:27:17,639
but the basic stages are forming,

597
00:27:17,640 --> 00:27:20,240
storming, norming, performing,

598
00:27:20,240 --> 00:27:22,400
and then there's kind of one called adjourning.

599
00:27:22,400 --> 00:27:23,840
So basically what happens is

600
00:27:23,840 --> 00:27:27,040
when people first come together for an experience,

601
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:29,600
they're kind of excited to be there.

602
00:27:29,600 --> 00:27:32,280
They're trying to get a sense of what it's all about,

603
00:27:32,280 --> 00:27:33,960
who the other people are.

604
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:38,000
But then, as challenges set in

605
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,240
and as they have to cook together

606
00:27:40,240 --> 00:27:41,680
and sleep in tents together

607
00:27:41,680 --> 00:27:43,520
and hike long miles together,

608
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:45,800
they get into a storming stage

609
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:47,840
where there's some conflict

610
00:27:47,840 --> 00:27:49,960
amongst group members potentially.

611
00:27:49,960 --> 00:27:52,159
And that's actually not bad.

612
00:27:52,159 --> 00:27:54,120
It's okay to have some of that conflict

613
00:27:54,120 --> 00:27:56,680
because it helps to establish

614
00:27:56,680 --> 00:27:58,919
how the group's gonna work together.

615
00:27:58,919 --> 00:28:00,800
But if you can help facilitate that

616
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:02,639
by setting some of those

617
00:28:02,639 --> 00:28:05,320
expedition behavior expectations early,

618
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:06,240
that's helpful.

619
00:28:07,240 --> 00:28:10,040
Then you move into kind of a norming phase,

620
00:28:10,040 --> 00:28:15,040
which is figuring out how we work together. well,

621
00:28:16,600 --> 00:28:18,240
Leaving the fighting, the storming,

622
00:28:18,240 --> 00:28:21,200
the argument behind but working better together.

623
00:28:21,200 --> 00:28:22,840
And then from that norming

624
00:28:22,840 --> 00:28:24,800
you get into a performing stage

625
00:28:24,800 --> 00:28:28,040
where the group can really really work well together.

626
00:28:28,040 --> 00:28:29,720
And not every group gets there.

627
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:31,200
It depends on the leadership,

628
00:28:31,200 --> 00:28:32,520
the activity you're doing,

629
00:28:32,520 --> 00:28:35,040
the length of the program, the participants.

630
00:28:35,040 --> 00:28:36,399
and then the adjourning phase

631
00:28:36,399 --> 00:28:39,760
is kind of the hey, we're leaving

632
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:41,120
and people starting to realize

633
00:28:41,120 --> 00:28:44,240
that this isn't gonna go on forever

634
00:28:44,240 --> 00:28:47,680
and that people are gonna go and sort of separate

635
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:49,200
at the end of the trip

636
00:28:49,200 --> 00:28:51,360
And at each stage of this.

637
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:54,080
there's no science to this,

638
00:28:54,080 --> 00:28:55,600
It's really more of an art.

639
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:57,760
but it's being able to kind of

640
00:28:57,760 --> 00:29:00,200
to step back as a leader

641
00:29:00,200 --> 00:29:04,840
and not impose your own perspective, as much

642
00:29:04,840 --> 00:29:07,360
as try and look at the group

643
00:29:07,360 --> 00:29:09,520
and how people are interacting

644
00:29:09,520 --> 00:29:13,040
and even how they physically look.

645
00:29:13,040 --> 00:29:15,560
Are they kind of lazily,

646
00:29:15,560 --> 00:29:17,879
literally dipping their paddle in the canoe?

647
00:29:17,879 --> 00:29:20,320
Are they did they have their head down

648
00:29:20,320 --> 00:29:22,040
and they're looking miserable

649
00:29:22,040 --> 00:29:22,879
backpacking?

650
00:29:22,879 --> 00:29:25,320
Are they kind of determined looking?

651
00:29:25,320 --> 00:29:28,440
And those little observations

652
00:29:28,440 --> 00:29:31,120
about individuals and groups

653
00:29:31,120 --> 00:29:33,320
can really help you understand

654
00:29:33,320 --> 00:29:34,960
who needs support,

655
00:29:34,960 --> 00:29:39,040
how you might be able to encourage certain people,

656
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:41,520
how you who in the group's doing really well

657
00:29:41,520 --> 00:29:44,360
and you might be able to you task them

658
00:29:44,360 --> 00:29:46,480
with helping other group members

659
00:29:46,480 --> 00:29:48,120
who are struggling a little more.

660
00:29:48,120 --> 00:29:50,200
So that assessment thing-

661
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:53,760
there's no rule book for it,

662
00:29:53,760 --> 00:29:55,760
but it's being able to kind of

663
00:29:55,760 --> 00:29:58,080
leave behind your own perspective,

664
00:29:58,080 --> 00:30:01,720
your own perhaps tired state,

665
00:30:01,720 --> 00:30:05,680
struggling, hiking or your own viewpoint

666
00:30:05,680 --> 00:30:08,760
and really look objectively at what's going on in the group

667
00:30:08,760 --> 00:30:11,720
and try and help the group

668
00:30:11,720 --> 00:30:14,280
move through some of their challenges

669
00:30:14,280 --> 00:30:17,240
and work together to achieve a greater good.

670
00:30:17,240 --> 00:30:19,440
Scouters will be pretty familiar-

671
00:30:19,440 --> 00:30:20,960
well, at least ones who have been through

672
00:30:20,960 --> 00:30:22,240
some of the more advanced training

673
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:23,680
like wood badge and things

674
00:30:23,680 --> 00:30:25,520
would be- are gonna be real familiar

675
00:30:25,520 --> 00:30:27,280
with the storming, norming and forming

676
00:30:27,280 --> 00:30:29,200
and the forming, norming, storming

677
00:30:29,200 --> 00:30:31,600
or the inks and those dynamics.

678
00:30:31,600 --> 00:30:35,640
but just being cognizant of these processes

679
00:30:35,640 --> 00:30:38,600
is such an incredibly important thing.

680
00:30:38,600 --> 00:30:40,400
When you're working in that

681
00:30:40,400 --> 00:30:43,240
kind of group development phase

682
00:30:43,240 --> 00:30:44,280
and you're getting through that,

683
00:30:44,280 --> 00:30:47,600
you're also minimizing a lot of the,

684
00:30:47,600 --> 00:30:49,200
a lot of possible difficulties

685
00:30:49,200 --> 00:30:50,760
you're gonna run into.

686
00:30:50,760 --> 00:30:52,440
I wanna I wanna spend some time

687
00:30:52,440 --> 00:30:55,680
talking about a very important part of the book

688
00:30:55,680 --> 00:30:57,520
which is risk management.

689
00:30:57,520 --> 00:30:58,920
Here's what I think, Alex.

690
00:30:58,920 --> 00:31:00,280
I think a lot of us run into it

691
00:31:00,280 --> 00:31:01,720
in our professional lives

692
00:31:01,720 --> 00:31:05,840
and there's somebody who is like the risk manager

693
00:31:05,840 --> 00:31:07,480
and nobody really wants to see that guy

694
00:31:07,520 --> 00:31:09,000
come through their office door.

695
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:12,640
but I'm a big advocate of understanding,

696
00:31:12,640 --> 00:31:15,560
especially in these kind of outdoor adventures,

697
00:31:15,560 --> 00:31:17,400
the role of risk management,

698
00:31:17,400 --> 00:31:19,160
and I know that you spend a lot of time

699
00:31:19,160 --> 00:31:20,440
assessing this stuff.

700
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:22,560
So give me a starter course

701
00:31:22,560 --> 00:31:24,200
in a couple of minutes here.

702
00:31:24,200 --> 00:31:27,040
What if? if I'm headed off

703
00:31:27,040 --> 00:31:28,680
to take a group canoeing for a week

704
00:31:28,680 --> 00:31:29,960
or backpacking for a week,

705
00:31:29,960 --> 00:31:32,760
what is it that I need to be thinking about?

706
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:34,000
that I might not know?

707
00:31:34,000 --> 00:31:36,160
Here's summary: there is that a lot of people

708
00:31:36,160 --> 00:31:39,560
or don't really wanna deal with. the risk manager

709
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:43,320
is very accurate, and this is primarily

710
00:31:43,320 --> 00:31:46,160
what I do for a living actually

711
00:31:46,160 --> 00:31:50,440
is help. outdoor programs deal with safety

712
00:31:50,440 --> 00:31:53,200
and, to some degree, liability issues,

713
00:31:53,200 --> 00:31:55,840
And one of the things that's really important to me,

714
00:31:55,840 --> 00:31:58,880
before like delving into the course idea,

715
00:31:58,880 --> 00:32:03,760
is that as a culture we're very risk averse,

716
00:32:03,760 --> 00:32:06,000
We don't like danger,

717
00:32:06,000 --> 00:32:08,160
and I think it's really important-

718
00:32:08,160 --> 00:32:11,280
and I think that the scouts actually really help

719
00:32:11,280 --> 00:32:13,840
to encourage this in our society a little-

720
00:32:13,840 --> 00:32:16,800
to understand that life

